## One prompt for everything

<At levels="simple">

In <ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" />, one agent did every step of the job in one conversation.
Every tool result stayed in its prompt, so each call read more than the last. On a big job, like
comparing ten products, the prompt fills up with search results the agent no longer needs.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The agent in <ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" /> did every step in one conversation. Every tool
result stayed in its prompt, so each call read more than the one before. On a big job, such as
comparing ten products, the prompt fills with search results the agent has already used, and the
harness has to drop or summarize them, as in <ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" />.

</At>

## A lead and its helpers

<At levels="simple">

Another way is to split the job. A **lead** agent breaks it into parts and writes a short task for
each. Each part goes to a <GlossaryPopover term="subagent">subagent</GlossaryPopover>, a helper that
starts with a fresh prompt holding only its task, makes its own tool calls, and sends back a short
summary. The lead only ever reads the summaries. Anthropic's research feature works like
this.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Another way is to split the job. A **lead** agent breaks it into parts and writes a short task for
each. Each part goes to a <GlossaryPopover term="subagent">subagent</GlossaryPopover>, a helper that
is an agent loop of its own: it starts with a fresh prompt holding only its task, makes its own
tool calls, and sends back a short summary. The lead never sees the helpers' tool results, only
their summaries. Anthropic's research feature works this way: a lead agent plans the search and
starts helpers that each explore one part of the question with their own context, then pass back
what matters.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />

</At>

Try it, with made-up prompt sizes:

<SplitTheWork />

<At levels="simple">

With three parts of three tool calls each, one agent's last prompt is 1,110 tokens long. Split up,
no prompt is over 540, and because the helpers can work at the same time, fewer calls wait for the
one before.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

With three subtasks of three tool calls each, one agent makes 10 calls and its last prompt is
1,110 tokens long. Split up, there are 14 calls, but no prompt is over 540 tokens, and because the
helpers can run at the same time, only 6 calls wait for the one before. Here the split version even
reads fewer tokens in all, 4,260 against 7,050, because no prompt carries every result.

</At>

## What splitting costs

<At levels="simple">

The toy does the same work either way, but real helpers search more widely, so splitting usually
costs more. In Anthropic's data, a system of several agents used about 15 times as many tokens as a
chat. It paid off on broad questions that split into separate parts.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />
It works less well when every part depends on the others, as in most coding, and the lead has to
describe each part clearly, or helpers repeat each other's work.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The toy keeps the work the same either way. Real systems don't: their helpers search more widely,
so they spend far more tokens. In Anthropic's data, an agent used about 4 times as many tokens as a
chat, and a system of several agents about 15 times. In return, their lead with helpers did 90.2%
better than a single agent on their own research test, especially on broad questions that split
into independent parts.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />

It works less well when every part needs the same shared context or depends on the others, which
Anthropic says is true of most coding tasks. The lead also has to describe each task well: given
vague tasks, their helpers repeated each other's searches or left gaps.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />
And each helper can have its own instructions and its own tools,<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />
so a helper that only needs to read can be given tools that can't change anything, in the spirit
of <ChapterRef to="trust-and-guardrails" />.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### The arithmetic

One agent doing $N$ subtasks of $s$ tool calls makes $K = Ns + 1$ calls and, as in the agent loop,
reads $K\,p_0 + a\,K(K-1)/2$ tokens in all, which grows with the square of the work. Split up, the
lead's two calls read $p_0$ and $p_0 + Nc$, where $c$ is what each handoff and summary add, and
each helper's $s + 1$ calls read $(s+1)\,h + a\,s(s+1)/2$, where $h$ is a helper's first prompt.
The total,

$$
2p_0 + Nc + N\left[(s+1)\,h + a\,\frac{s(s+1)}{2}\right],
$$

grows only in proportion to $N$. The widget uses $p_0 = 300$, $a = 90$, $h = 150$, and $c = 80$.

Anthropic found that how many tokens a system spends explains most of how well it does. On
BrowseComp, a test of finding hard-to-find facts on the web, token use alone explained 80% of the
differences in score, and the number of tool calls and the choice of model most of the
rest.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" /> Splitting the work across helpers is one way to spend
more. Their lead also waits for each round of helpers to finish before going on: simpler, but one
slow helper holds up the rest.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A lead agent can split a job and hand each part to a subagent that starts with a fresh prompt and
  reports back a short summary.
- No prompt has to hold every result, and helpers can run at the same time.
- Real systems spend many more tokens this way. It pays off on broad jobs that split into
  independent parts, less on tightly linked ones like most coding.

<At levels="deep">

- Split up, the tokens read grow in proportion to the number of subtasks; one agent's grow with
  the square of the work.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** One agent or several, how do you know the job was done right?
